Let me throw in my two cents here, seeing as I am a variant developer and I=
 feel as though I should explain why I did what I did.

On Sunday, 8 April 2012 18:32:53 UTC-6, Jonadab the Unsightly One  wrote:
> [...]
>  A. Not many major goals you can work on.
Once you've entered Gehennom, your general objectives (assuming you've comp=
leted the Quest by then, which is the case in my own play and in most other=
 players I've observed) are to collect the Candelabrum and Book and map the=
 invocation square so as to be able to perform the Invocation and collect t=
he Amulet.

I think it can be agreed that challenging the Wizard of Yendor for the Book=
 of the Dead is an interesting experience in itself, be it through storming=
 his Tower or through one of the various tricks to summon him outside of hi=
s tower without doing so. In contrast, claiming the Candelabrum in vanilla =
is an anti-climax, as Vlad is a pushover.

Thus, at a bare minimum, Vlad himself needs to be improved upon; his Tower =
could possibly use some changes as well (though how that would be done isn'=
t something I've thought of). Indeed, most variants I'm aware of buff Vlad =
considerably in some fashion (equipping him with a life-draining weapon of =
some sort seems to be popular).

>  B. Lots of levels that take a long time to traverse EVERY TIME.
>  C. Lots of nearly-identical levels that take a long time to explore.
I'll take these together - having a near-complete set of maze levels just d=
oesn't cut it.

When I was first developing GruntHack five and a half years ago, the genera=
l approach I thought that I wanted to take was to make Gehennom more, well,=
 hellish (given that it evolved out of a part of the dungeon that was once =
*called* Hell). Thus, I viewed lava-filled caverns as a good approach to ta=
ke. (Later, I began adding in additional things such as stinking clouds occ=
asionally generated by the lava, which really adds to the atmosphere).

Of course, this still has the problem of most levels being identical. The s=
olution that I ultimately decided on was to add in a few more special level=
s (more demon fortresses); not having to navigate a maze around the origina=
l fortresses improves navigation through those levels considerably, and mos=
t of the new fortresses contain large wide-open areas.

To be fair, I don't necessarily view the lack of maneuverability through th=
e mazes on ascent to be a problem; most experienced players I've seen play =
through vanilla Gehennom (myself included) will either:

a) dig passages between the stairs;

b) make judicious use of the travel command when traversing several levels =
in a hurry, which significantly reduces the amount of time;

c) level-teleport (before acquiring the Amulet, of course); or

d) some combination of the above.

Thus, I don't see there being a problem after the levels have been mapped o=
ut - instead, I view the problem being the mapping of the levels themselves=
, which seems simply to be an exercise in filling time. Thus, I still view =
having the more open level layouts that are present in GruntHack as a good =
thing.

>  D. Annoying enemies.  Glob flies, Worf would call them.
One of the key problems here, I think, is that the enemies that are *suppos=
ed* to present a major challenge - to wit, the demon lords and princes, and=
 Vlad the Impaler (covered earlier) - don't. It would be a good thing, then=
, to determine how those battles could be improved upon.

This being (a new version of) Hell, what would the greatest asset of a demo=
n prince / lord be? In my mind, it should be their ability to command lesse=
r demons and send them on the attack. Since even an experienced player fear=
s a summon storm, perhaps one possibility is to have demon princes / lords =
gate in minions more frequently.

Of course, that isn't necessarily very interesting if the player can easily=
 kill those lesser minions. As such, one additional possibility that has cr=
ossed my mind is to rethink the level of difficulty that regular demons sho=
uld have, and/or possibly consider adding more types of demons. (After all,=
 a hostile Archon is considered a fairly challenging foe under many circums=
tances; why aren't there any regular demons that could provide that?)

As an aside, while I was developing GruntHack, I concluded that the telepor=
ting behaviour the demon lords and princes (and other covetous monsters) ex=
hibit is a bug - one caused by simply reusing the code from the Wizard of Y=
endor wholesale, without consideration to the fact that most of the demon p=
rinces aren't supposed to be able to teleport (as evidenced by their monste=
r definitions). I think that addressing this has caused the battles against=
 the demon princes to make a little bit more sense and be slightly less ann=
oying and slightly more enjoyable, if less difficult; whether this is a ste=
p in the right direction is debatable, but I believe it is.

>  E. Not enough intesting things to find.
By the time a player has entered Gehennom, they probably have most if not a=
ll of their ascension kit at the ready and will be focused on completing th=
eir objectives rather than searching for treasure; thus, I don't view this =
as a priority.

>  F. Not many interesting dangers to face.
I think this has been covered adequately by my previous points.

Anyway, I hope this is insightful and/or thought-provoking for the rest of =
you.


